/*
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    The rules a utility class cannot say, and nothing else.

    ⚠ Every rule here is one for a reason a class name could not carry: a custom property the control
    library reads, a default for a *tag* rather than for an element, or a relationship between two
    states. Anything that is "this element, this property, this value" belongs in Shell.vxml as a
    utility — a rule in this file is a rule somebody has to find before they can change it.

    ⚠ **In `@layer components`, and the statement above the block is not optional.** Layer *order*
    belongs to whoever names a layer first, and this text is concatenated ahead of the generated
    utility sheet — so without that line `components` would be layer zero, `base` and `utilities`
    would be declared after it, and the ladder would come out `components, base, utilities` with
    `base` in the middle. Naming all three here says the order once; the generated sheet restates the
    same line and a re-declaration never moves a layer.

    ⚠ **This is an *author* sheet and the control theme is a *user-agent* one.** So nothing here has
    to out-specify anything: a plain `menu-bar { … }` beats `ControlTheme.vcss`'s own rule because of
    where it came from, not because of what it selects. The layer above settles this sheet's argument
    with its own utilities, and nothing else.

    ⚠ **Saving this file with the sample running repaints it.** `HotReloadWatcher` is watching this
    directory — see Program.cs — and a stylesheet reload keeps every element's identity, so the focus,
    the scroll offset and the docking arrangement all survive. That is the loop this sample is for.
*/

@layer base, components, utilities;

@layer components {

/* ── The window's own two elements ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    The root is the one element no markup owns — Program.cs puts two classes on it — so the ground
    colour is a token rather than a literal, and it is the same one UiApplicationOptions.Ground is
    given. Those two have to agree: the clear colour is what a `backdrop-filter` starts from, and a
    root painted a different shade would put a rectangle of the wrong colour under every glass panel.
*/
root {
    /*
        ⚠ The control theme's own token rather than `--color-chrome`, so that the View ▸ Toggle Dark
        command actually changes the window's ground and not only the panels on it. `root.dark` in
        `ControlTheme.vcss` redefines `--surface-sunken`; a literal here — or this sample's own
        `--color-chrome`, which has one value — would leave a light window behind a dark interface.
    */
    background-color: var(--surface-sunken);
}

/* ── Defaults per tag, which a utility cannot express ───────────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    ⚠ A menu bar must not stretch. It is a flex item in a column, so without this it takes an equal
    share of the height and the docking host gets what is left — which is a menu bar half the window
    tall, and looks like a layout engine bug rather than a missing line.
*/
menu-bar {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 2px 4px;
    border-width: 0 0 1px 0;
    border-color: var(--border);
    background-color: var(--surface);
}

/*
    ProgressBar's fill takes the element's foreground when nothing sets --fill-color, which is what
    lets a `text-brand` in the markup colour a bar. The *track* has no such fallback, so it is set
    once here rather than on every bar.
*/
progress-bar {
    --track-color: #2a2e36;
}

/*
    ⚠ **The three panel components' host elements, and this rule is not optional.**

    A component draws into a host element of its own — `@tag hierarchy-panel` is why that element is
    called what it is — so what a `DockPanel` actually contains is not the `<TreeView>` in the markup
    but this, with the tree inside it. Nothing styles an element no file mentions, and CSS's initial
    `flex-direction` is `row` with `flex-grow` unset, so without these two declarations the host comes
    out content-sized in both axes and the panel is *empty*: the tree is there, laid out at zero
    height, drawn as nothing.

    ⚠ It is worth knowing what that failure looks like, because it looks like the wrong thing. The
    hierarchy panel showed a blank rectangle, which reads as the virtualiser having realised no rows
    — a plausible and completely wrong diagnosis. The tree had realised its rows correctly into a box
    zero pixels tall.

    ⚠ `min-height: 0` is the third one, and it bites later rather than immediately. A flex item's
    minimum size is its content, so a scroller inside one of these cannot shrink below the height of
    everything in it: the panel grows past the window instead of scrolling, and the scrollbar it
    draws has nothing to do.
*/
hierarchy-panel,
inspector-panel,
gallery-panel {
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 0px;
}

/*
    And the controls inside them, for the same reason one level down: a `DockPanel` is a column, and
    a tree or a grid that did not grow would draw at its content height with the panel's background
    below it.
*/
tree-view,
property-grid {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

property-grid {
    padding: 8px;
}

/* ── A relationship between two states, which is the other thing a class cannot say ─────────── */

/*
    ⚠ The gallery's cards lift on hover, and the transition is declared rather than animated by hand.
    `UiDocument.Tick` is what advances it — a frame loop that forgot the clock leaves a declared
    transition holding the property at the value it was leaving instead of moving to the new one, so
    this rule is also the cheapest visible check that the loop is right.
*/
.gallery card {
    transition: background-color 120ms ease-out;
}

.gallery card:hover {
    background-color: var(--surface-raised);
}

}
